From the k60 re-review (non-blocking; such entries never match, so they grant no access). (1) internal/config/config.go:919 — a last label of 0x plus more than 16 hex digits overflows the number parser, is treated as a name, and http://a.0xffffffffffffffffffff loads; a browser reads it as IPv4 and rejects the URL. Decide 'numeric' from the characters, not from the parse error. (2) config.go:851 — Go's url.Parse accepts < and > (and other WHATWG forbidden host code points) in a host, so http://a<b.example and chrome-extension://a<b load though no browser can send them; refuse forbidden host code points. (3) internal/config/mcp_test.go:150 — the comment 'An extension's host is opaque to a browser' holds for the URL Standard, not for Chromium; say 'to the URL Standard' or give the real reason (no extension id is numeric). Lane: unreagent-worker; after k60 lands.

