Changed (k166 part, `internal/config/config.go`):
1. `browserIPv4`: whether the last label is numeric now depends only on its characters, as in the URL Standard's ends-in-a-number check: all decimal digits, or `0x`/`0X` followed only by hex digits (a bare `0x` counts). It used to follow the `ipv4Number` parse error (k60, unreleased). `http://a.0xffffffffffffffffffff` and `http://0xffffffffffffffffffff` are now refused with "numeric host but no IPv4 address a browser accepts". Every label the old rule counted as numeric still counts, so nothing that was refused now loads.
2. `validOrigin`: a new `forbiddenHostChars` holds the URL Standard's full forbidden host code point list. It applies to every non-bracketed host, for http(s) and for extension schemes alike: "no "<" allowed in a host, a browser refuses the URL". url.Parse (Go 1.26, probed) lets `<`, `>` and `]` through and rejects or splits on the rest. For http and https `%` is refused too, since the host is a domain and `%` is a forbidden *domain* code point: `https://a%25b.example` decodes to `a%b.example`. Bracketed hosts skip this check (they still go to `canonicalIP`, so the zone message for `[fe80::1%25eth0]` stays).
3. `mcp_test.go` comment on `moz-extension://2130706433`: "To the URL Standard an extension's host is opaque: no IPv4 reading. Chromium parses its own extension scheme like http, but no extension id is numeric."

My decision: an extension host keeps `%` (`chrome-extension://a%25b` loads), because the URL Standard keeps percent-encoding in an opaque host. I did not pin this in a test.

Tests: `TestMCPAllowedOriginsRefused` adds `http://a.0xffffffffffffffffffff`, `http://0xffffffffffffffffffff`, `http://ide.example.0X1` (the last pins the uppercase prefix and was already refused), plus `http://a<b.example`, `https://a>b.example`, `http://a]b.example`, `https://a%25b.example`, `chrome-extension://a<b`, `chrome-extension://a>b` and `moz-extension://a]b`. `TestMCPAllowedOriginsAccepted` adds `http://ide.example.0xg`: 0x followed by a non-hex character is a name to a browser. Every origin the test already allowed still loads. Red before the fix: the 2 overflow cases and the 7 forbidden-character cases loaded.

